1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,494
Drive near an airport or across a

2
00:00:02,494 --> 00:00:04,912
big river valley and you may see

3
00:00:04,912 --> 00:00:08,162
giant colored balls hanging on power lines,

4
00:00:08,162 --> 00:00:10,051
orange, white and yellow,

5
00:00:10,051 --> 00:00:11,714
high above the ground.

6
00:00:11,894 --> 00:00:14,063
They look like toys someone threw up

7
00:00:14,063 --> 00:00:14,425
there.

8
00:00:14,605 --> 00:00:15,360
They are not.

9
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:17,939
Each one may be saving a pilot's

10
00:00:17,939 --> 00:00:18,314
life.

11
00:00:18,494 --> 00:00:21,632
Today, eleven parts of a high voltage

12
00:00:21,632 --> 00:00:24,684
power line, and why each one exists,

13
00:00:24,684 --> 00:00:26,888
starting with those balls.

14
00:00:27,239 --> 00:00:30,949
Power lines carry electricity from power plants

15
00:00:30,949 --> 00:00:34,817
to cities, sometimes over hundreds of kilometers,

16
00:00:34,817 --> 00:00:37,502
at hundreds of thousands of volts.

17
00:00:37,682 --> 00:00:39,504
They cross mountains,

18
00:00:39,504 --> 00:00:41,153
rivers and forests.

19
00:00:41,333 --> 00:00:43,299
They are hit by wind,

20
00:00:43,299 --> 00:00:45,640
ice, lightning and birds.

21
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:48,514
And almost nothing on them is decoration.

22
00:00:48,694 --> 00:00:51,209
Every strange object you see on a

23
00:00:51,209 --> 00:00:53,495
power line is there to solve a

24
00:00:53,495 --> 00:00:54,104
problem.

25
00:00:54,455 --> 00:00:57,280
As always, every fact comes from a

26
00:00:57,280 --> 00:00:59,774
named source, power companies,

27
00:00:59,774 --> 00:01:04,511
aviation authorities, and official investigation reports,

28
00:01:04,511 --> 00:01:07,005
all listed in the description.

29
00:01:07,185 --> 00:01:09,101
Let's start with the balls.

30
00:01:09,451 --> 00:01:11,896
Why are there big colored balls on

31
00:01:11,896 --> 00:01:13,119
some power lines?

32
00:01:13,299 --> 00:01:16,550
Because pilots cannot see the wires.

33
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:19,435
A thin cable stretched across a valley

34
00:01:19,435 --> 00:01:21,783
is almost invisible from the air,

35
00:01:21,783 --> 00:01:23,278
even in good weather.

36
00:01:23,458 --> 00:01:25,249
Low flying aircraft,

37
00:01:25,249 --> 00:01:29,548
like helicopters, crop dusters and small planes,

38
00:01:29,548 --> 00:01:33,131
have crashed into power lines they never

39
00:01:33,131 --> 00:01:33,489
saw.

40
00:01:33,669 --> 00:01:36,368
The balls make the wires visible from

41
00:01:36,368 --> 00:01:37,024
far away.

42
00:01:37,375 --> 00:01:39,861
They are called aerial marker balls,

43
00:01:39,861 --> 00:01:42,485
and aviation rules say where they must

44
00:01:42,485 --> 00:01:42,692
go.

45
00:01:42,872 --> 00:01:44,847
Usually near airports,

46
00:01:44,847 --> 00:01:47,181
across rivers and canyons,

47
00:01:47,181 --> 00:01:49,246
and on very high spans.

48
00:01:49,426 --> 00:01:51,038
In the United States,

49
00:01:51,038 --> 00:01:53,342
they can be up to about ninety

50
00:01:53,342 --> 00:01:55,646
centimeters across, in orange,

51
00:01:55,646 --> 00:01:56,874
white or yellow,

52
00:01:56,874 --> 00:01:59,869
and the colors alternate along the line

53
00:01:59,869 --> 00:02:02,403
so they stand out against the sky

54
00:02:02,403 --> 00:02:03,555
and the ground.

55
00:02:03,906 --> 00:02:05,472
The balls are hollow,

56
00:02:05,472 --> 00:02:08,158
made of light plastic or fiberglass,

57
00:02:08,158 --> 00:02:10,545
so they add little weight to the

58
00:02:10,545 --> 00:02:10,918
line.

59
00:02:11,098 --> 00:02:14,003
They are clamped on by line workers,

60
00:02:14,003 --> 00:02:15,939
often from a helicopter.

61
00:02:16,119 --> 00:02:19,043
A few kilograms of plastic make a

62
00:02:19,043 --> 00:02:21,878
deadly cable impossible to miss.

63
00:02:22,228 --> 00:02:25,075
Why do power lines have small dumbbell

64
00:02:25,075 --> 00:02:27,998
shaped weights hanging near the towers?

65
00:02:28,178 --> 00:02:30,327
They are Stockbridge dampers,

66
00:02:30,327 --> 00:02:33,216
invented in the nineteen twenties by an

67
00:02:33,216 --> 00:02:35,736
engineer named George Stockbridge.

68
00:02:35,916 --> 00:02:38,731
They stop the wires from vibrating themselves

69
00:02:38,731 --> 00:02:39,294
to death.

70
00:02:39,474 --> 00:02:42,470
A steady breeze blowing across a cable

71
00:02:42,470 --> 00:02:44,441
sheds tiny swirls of air,

72
00:02:44,441 --> 00:02:47,201
which make the cable vibrate up and

73
00:02:47,201 --> 00:02:48,384
down very fast,

74
00:02:48,384 --> 00:02:50,039
with small movements.

75
00:02:50,390 --> 00:02:53,071
This is called aeolian vibration,

76
00:02:53,071 --> 00:02:55,672
after the Greek god of the wind.

77
00:02:55,852 --> 00:02:58,432
It is too small to see easily,

78
00:02:58,432 --> 00:03:00,066
but it never stops.

79
00:03:00,246 --> 00:03:01,852
Over months and years,

80
00:03:01,852 --> 00:03:04,407
the bending at the clamps tires the

81
00:03:04,407 --> 00:03:07,108
metal strands, and they can break one

82
00:03:07,108 --> 00:03:07,619
by one.

83
00:03:07,799 --> 00:03:10,651
A broken conductor can drop a live

84
00:03:10,651 --> 00:03:12,245
wire to the ground.

85
00:03:12,596 --> 00:03:14,808
The two weights hang on a short

86
00:03:14,808 --> 00:03:16,949
piece of flexible steel cable.

87
00:03:17,129 --> 00:03:19,055
When the wire vibrates,

88
00:03:19,055 --> 00:03:22,238
the weights swing and flex that cable,

89
00:03:22,238 --> 00:03:25,755
turning the vibration energy into a little

90
00:03:25,755 --> 00:03:26,173
heat.

91
00:03:26,353 --> 00:03:28,696
It is one of the simplest and

92
00:03:28,696 --> 00:03:32,573
most successful inventions in power engineering,

93
00:03:32,573 --> 00:03:34,915
and it is still used all over

94
00:03:34,915 --> 00:03:35,723
the world.

95
00:03:36,073 --> 00:03:39,164
Why do the biggest power lines use

96
00:03:39,164 --> 00:03:40,437
groups of two,

97
00:03:40,437 --> 00:03:42,255
three or four wires,

98
00:03:42,255 --> 00:03:44,710
held apart by metal frames?

99
00:03:44,890 --> 00:03:47,623
Because one thick wire would waste energy

100
00:03:47,623 --> 00:03:48,489
into the air.

101
00:03:48,669 --> 00:03:50,486
At very high voltages,

102
00:03:50,486 --> 00:03:53,705
the electric field around a single wire

103
00:03:53,705 --> 00:03:56,594
is so strong that it rips electrons

104
00:03:56,594 --> 00:03:58,410
off the air molecules.

105
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:01,168
That is called corona discharge.

106
00:04:01,348 --> 00:04:02,793
It wastes power,

107
00:04:02,793 --> 00:04:06,677
makes noise, and causes radio interference.

108
00:04:07,027 --> 00:04:09,811
Splitting each phase into a bundle of

109
00:04:09,811 --> 00:04:13,197
smaller wires spreads the electric field out,

110
00:04:13,197 --> 00:04:16,207
like making the wire act thicker without

111
00:04:16,207 --> 00:04:17,034
the weight.

112
00:04:17,214 --> 00:04:19,919
The bundle also carries more current.

113
00:04:20,099 --> 00:04:21,302
The metal frames,

114
00:04:21,302 --> 00:04:23,919
called spacers, keep the wires at the

115
00:04:23,919 --> 00:04:26,395
right distance, so they do not slap

116
00:04:26,395 --> 00:04:28,517
together in the wind or when a

117
00:04:28,517 --> 00:04:31,559
short circuit pulls them toward each other.

118
00:04:31,909 --> 00:04:32,950
On a damp day,

119
00:04:32,950 --> 00:04:35,853
you can sometimes hear a crackling buzz

120
00:04:35,853 --> 00:04:37,714
under high voltage lines.

121
00:04:37,894 --> 00:04:39,166
That is corona,

122
00:04:39,166 --> 00:04:42,305
the air itself conducting a tiny bit.

123
00:04:42,485 --> 00:04:45,492
The bundles and spacers keep it small.

124
00:04:45,672 --> 00:04:48,108
Without them, the buzz would be much

125
00:04:48,108 --> 00:04:50,409
louder and the losses much higher.

126
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:54,087
Why do power line insulators look like

127
00:04:54,087 --> 00:04:57,064
stacks of glass or ceramic plates?

128
00:04:57,244 --> 00:05:00,057
Because they must keep hundreds of thousands

129
00:05:00,057 --> 00:05:02,294
of volts away from the steel tower.

130
00:05:02,474 --> 00:05:04,681
Each disc can hold back a certain

131
00:05:04,681 --> 00:05:07,155
voltage, so the higher the voltage of

132
00:05:07,155 --> 00:05:09,227
the line, the more discs in the

133
00:05:09,227 --> 00:05:09,696
string.

134
00:05:09,876 --> 00:05:12,314
You can roughly guess a line's voltage

135
00:05:12,314 --> 00:05:13,405
by counting them.

136
00:05:13,755 --> 00:05:16,042
The wavy shape is important.

137
00:05:16,222 --> 00:05:18,648
Dirt, salt and rain can form a

138
00:05:18,648 --> 00:05:20,589
wet path on the surface,

139
00:05:20,589 --> 00:05:23,420
and electricity can creep along it.

140
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,119
The ridges under each disc make the

141
00:05:26,119 --> 00:05:28,639
path along the surface much longer,

142
00:05:28,639 --> 00:05:30,510
and keep part of it dry in

143
00:05:30,510 --> 00:05:33,173
the rain, so the current cannot sneak

144
00:05:33,173 --> 00:05:33,677
across.

145
00:05:34,028 --> 00:05:36,977
Glass discs have a clever safety feature.

146
00:05:37,157 --> 00:05:39,378
If one is damaged by a bullet

147
00:05:39,378 --> 00:05:40,680
or a power surge,

148
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:43,360
it shatters completely and visibly,

149
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:46,271
while the metal parts keep holding the

150
00:05:46,271 --> 00:05:46,653
line.

151
00:05:46,833 --> 00:05:49,719
Inspectors flying by can see the missing

152
00:05:49,719 --> 00:05:50,874
disc right away.

153
00:05:51,054 --> 00:05:54,012
Many new lines use long rubber polymer

154
00:05:54,012 --> 00:05:57,904
insulators, which are lighter and resist vandalism

155
00:05:57,904 --> 00:05:58,449
better.

156
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:01,466
Why do some insulators have shiny metal

157
00:06:01,466 --> 00:06:03,107
rings around their ends?

158
00:06:03,287 --> 00:06:05,139
They are corona rings,

159
00:06:05,139 --> 00:06:07,327
also called grading rings.

160
00:06:07,507 --> 00:06:09,811
At the end of an insulator,

161
00:06:09,811 --> 00:06:13,651
the electric field gets concentrated on sharp

162
00:06:13,651 --> 00:06:16,040
edges and small metal parts.

163
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:20,311
That concentrated field causes corona discharge,

164
00:06:20,311 --> 00:06:24,146
which slowly damages the insulator and wastes

165
00:06:24,146 --> 00:06:24,742
energy.

166
00:06:25,093 --> 00:06:28,252
A smooth, round ring spreads the field

167
00:06:28,252 --> 00:06:30,414
out over a larger surface,

168
00:06:30,414 --> 00:06:32,992
the same way the bundles do for

169
00:06:32,992 --> 00:06:33,823
the wires.

170
00:06:34,003 --> 00:06:35,737
On polymer insulators,

171
00:06:35,737 --> 00:06:37,707
this protects the rubber,

172
00:06:37,707 --> 00:06:40,544
which can be burned by constant tiny

173
00:06:40,544 --> 00:06:41,410
discharges.

174
00:06:41,590 --> 00:06:43,913
On the highest voltage lines,

175
00:06:43,913 --> 00:06:46,396
you will see rings at both ends

176
00:06:46,396 --> 00:06:47,918
of every insulator.

177
00:06:48,268 --> 00:06:50,020
It is a rule you see all

178
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:52,064
over high voltage equipment.

179
00:06:52,244 --> 00:06:53,945
Sharp points are bad,

180
00:06:53,945 --> 00:06:55,808
smooth curves are good.

181
00:06:55,988 --> 00:06:59,131
That is why substations are full of

182
00:06:59,131 --> 00:07:01,735
round tubes, domes and rings.

183
00:07:01,915 --> 00:07:05,674
Electricity at these voltages leaks from anything

184
00:07:05,674 --> 00:07:06,211
pointy.

185
00:07:06,561 --> 00:07:09,010
Why do many power towers have one

186
00:07:09,010 --> 00:07:11,756
or two thin wires running along their

187
00:07:11,756 --> 00:07:14,130
very tops, above the main wires?

188
00:07:14,310 --> 00:07:16,145
They are shield wires,

189
00:07:16,145 --> 00:07:17,480
or ground wires.

190
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:20,136
They are connected to the steel towers

191
00:07:20,136 --> 00:07:21,244
and to the earth,

192
00:07:21,244 --> 00:07:22,873
and they catch lightning.

193
00:07:23,053 --> 00:07:25,492
A strike on the shield wire flows

194
00:07:25,492 --> 00:07:28,374
down the towers into the ground instead

195
00:07:28,374 --> 00:07:30,739
of hitting the live wires below.

196
00:07:31,089 --> 00:07:33,887
Without them, a lightning strike on a

197
00:07:33,887 --> 00:07:36,459
power wire would send a huge surge

198
00:07:36,459 --> 00:07:37,594
along the line,

199
00:07:37,594 --> 00:07:41,073
damaging equipment and tripping the power off.

200
00:07:41,253 --> 00:07:43,942
The shield wires create a kind of

201
00:07:43,942 --> 00:07:46,958
protective tent above the conductors,

202
00:07:46,958 --> 00:07:49,810
like an umbrella against lightning.

203
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:53,122
Many modern shield wires have a hidden

204
00:07:53,122 --> 00:07:53,979
second job.

205
00:07:54,159 --> 00:07:57,526
Inside, they carry optical fibers.

206
00:07:57,706 --> 00:08:00,897
Power companies use them to send control

207
00:08:00,897 --> 00:08:04,007
signals, and some rent the extra fibers

208
00:08:04,007 --> 00:08:05,682
to telecom companies.

209
00:08:05,862 --> 00:08:08,644
A wire meant to catch lightning also

210
00:08:08,644 --> 00:08:10,576
carries internet traffic.

211
00:08:10,926 --> 00:08:13,752
Why do some power lines have small

212
00:08:13,752 --> 00:08:17,160
spirals or flapping tags clipped on them?

213
00:08:17,340 --> 00:08:19,000
They are bird diverters.

214
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:21,667
Large birds, like cranes,

215
00:08:21,667 --> 00:08:24,352
swans, storks and bustards,

216
00:08:24,352 --> 00:08:27,932
often cannot see thin wires until it

217
00:08:27,932 --> 00:08:29,125
is too late,

218
00:08:29,125 --> 00:08:32,009
especially in fog or at dusk.

219
00:08:32,189 --> 00:08:35,758
Collisions with power lines kill huge numbers

220
00:08:35,758 --> 00:08:37,344
of birds every year.

221
00:08:37,695 --> 00:08:40,827
The diverters make the wires thicker and

222
00:08:40,827 --> 00:08:43,019
more visible, and some flap,

223
00:08:43,019 --> 00:08:44,742
glow or reflect light.

224
00:08:44,922 --> 00:08:48,062
Studies have found that marking lines can

225
00:08:48,062 --> 00:08:50,435
greatly reduce bird collisions,

226
00:08:50,435 --> 00:08:53,116
often cutting them by half or more,

227
00:08:53,116 --> 00:08:56,102
depending on the species and the place.

228
00:08:56,453 --> 00:08:59,692
Power companies also design towers so that

229
00:08:59,692 --> 00:09:02,622
birds landing on them cannot touch two

230
00:09:02,622 --> 00:09:03,702
wires at once,

231
00:09:03,702 --> 00:09:05,938
which would electrocute them.

232
00:09:06,118 --> 00:09:09,352
Some even build nest platforms on towers

233
00:09:09,352 --> 00:09:11,212
for storks and ospreys,

234
00:09:11,212 --> 00:09:13,961
giving them a safe place away from

235
00:09:13,961 --> 00:09:15,174
the live wires.

236
00:09:15,524 --> 00:09:18,208
Why do many power towers have barbed

237
00:09:18,208 --> 00:09:20,892
wire or spiked panels on their legs,

238
00:09:20,892 --> 00:09:23,128
a few meters above the ground?

239
00:09:23,308 --> 00:09:25,073
Because people climb them.

240
00:09:25,253 --> 00:09:28,632
Children, thrill seekers and thieves have climbed

241
00:09:28,632 --> 00:09:30,977
towers, and some have been killed.

242
00:09:31,157 --> 00:09:32,882
You do not have to touch a

243
00:09:32,882 --> 00:09:35,336
high voltage wire to be electrocuted.

244
00:09:35,516 --> 00:09:36,661
At these voltages,

245
00:09:36,661 --> 00:09:38,951
the current can jump through the air

246
00:09:38,951 --> 00:09:40,032
if you get close.

247
00:09:40,383 --> 00:09:43,459
The anti climbing devices make the first

248
00:09:43,459 --> 00:09:45,766
few meters very hard to climb,

249
00:09:45,766 --> 00:09:48,150
and danger signs warn of death.

250
00:09:48,330 --> 00:09:50,778
Line workers who need to go up

251
00:09:50,778 --> 00:09:54,041
use special access points and switch off

252
00:09:54,041 --> 00:09:57,060
or work with strict safety distances.

253
00:09:57,411 --> 00:10:00,742
Even kites and drones are dangerous near

254
00:10:00,742 --> 00:10:01,741
power lines.

255
00:10:01,921 --> 00:10:05,170
A wet kite string can conduct electricity,

256
00:10:05,170 --> 00:10:07,568
and a drone touching a wire can

257
00:10:07,568 --> 00:10:08,574
short it out.

258
00:10:08,754 --> 00:10:11,656
The safest rule is the simple one.

259
00:10:11,836 --> 00:10:14,894
Stay well away from anything attached to

260
00:10:14,894 --> 00:10:15,888
a power line.

261
00:10:16,238 --> 00:10:18,421
How can a line worker touch a

262
00:10:18,421 --> 00:10:21,733
power line carrying hundreds of thousands of

263
00:10:21,733 --> 00:10:23,087
volts and survive?

264
00:10:23,267 --> 00:10:26,141
Because current only flows when there is

265
00:10:26,141 --> 00:10:27,865
a difference in voltage.

266
00:10:28,045 --> 00:10:30,815
Workers on a platform hanging from a

267
00:10:30,815 --> 00:10:34,431
helicopter wear suits woven with metal threads.

268
00:10:34,611 --> 00:10:36,886
They first touch the wire with a

269
00:10:36,886 --> 00:10:38,663
metal wand, so that they,

270
00:10:38,663 --> 00:10:41,152
the suit and the helicopter rise to

271
00:10:41,152 --> 00:10:43,214
the same voltage as the line.

272
00:10:43,564 --> 00:10:46,123
Once they are at the same voltage,

273
00:10:46,123 --> 00:10:48,983
no current flows through their bodies,

274
00:10:48,983 --> 00:10:51,391
the same reason birds can sit on

275
00:10:51,391 --> 00:10:52,445
a single wire.

276
00:10:52,625 --> 00:10:55,084
The metal suit acts like a Faraday

277
00:10:55,084 --> 00:10:58,337
cage, keeping the electric field around them.

278
00:10:58,517 --> 00:11:00,753
They can then repair spacers,

279
00:11:00,753 --> 00:11:03,992
replace insulators or fix damaged strands,

280
00:11:03,992 --> 00:11:06,768
without switching off the power to a

281
00:11:06,768 --> 00:11:07,770
whole region.

282
00:11:08,121 --> 00:11:10,970
The dangerous moment is the connection,

283
00:11:10,970 --> 00:11:13,528
when a bright arc jumps between the

284
00:11:13,528 --> 00:11:14,843
wand and the wire.

285
00:11:15,023 --> 00:11:17,328
After that, the worker can sit on

286
00:11:17,328 --> 00:11:19,004
the line as if it were a

287
00:11:19,004 --> 00:11:19,423
bench.

288
00:11:19,603 --> 00:11:21,813
It is one of the most extreme

289
00:11:21,813 --> 00:11:23,185
jobs in the world,

290
00:11:23,185 --> 00:11:26,005
and it only works because the physics

291
00:11:26,005 --> 00:11:27,300
is exactly right.

292
00:11:27,651 --> 00:11:29,657
Why do power lines hang in a

293
00:11:29,657 --> 00:11:32,237
curve instead of being pulled tight?

294
00:11:32,417 --> 00:11:35,513
Because a perfectly tight wire would need

295
00:11:35,513 --> 00:11:38,383
enormous force, and it would snap when

296
00:11:38,383 --> 00:11:40,044
it shrank in the cold.

297
00:11:40,224 --> 00:11:42,825
So lines are hung with a planned

298
00:11:42,825 --> 00:11:43,150
sag.

299
00:11:43,330 --> 00:11:45,176
But that sag changes.

300
00:11:45,356 --> 00:11:47,415
When a line carries a lot of

301
00:11:47,415 --> 00:11:48,959
current, it heats up,

302
00:11:48,959 --> 00:11:50,283
the metal expands,

303
00:11:50,283 --> 00:11:52,194
and the wire droops lower.

304
00:11:52,545 --> 00:11:55,550
On the fourteenth of August two thousand

305
00:11:55,550 --> 00:11:56,677
three, in Ohio,

306
00:11:56,677 --> 00:11:59,833
hot heavily loaded lines sagged into trees

307
00:11:59,833 --> 00:12:01,786
that had not been trimmed.

308
00:12:01,966 --> 00:12:04,137
They shorted out one after another.

309
00:12:04,317 --> 00:12:06,400
Alarm software had failed,

310
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:09,043
so operators did not see what was

311
00:12:09,043 --> 00:12:09,844
happening.

312
00:12:10,024 --> 00:12:13,478
The failure spread into the largest blackout

313
00:12:13,478 --> 00:12:15,520
in North American history,

314
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:18,975
cutting power to about fifty million people.

315
00:12:19,325 --> 00:12:22,216
That is why power companies keep wide

316
00:12:22,216 --> 00:12:24,639
clear strips under their lines,

317
00:12:24,639 --> 00:12:26,592
cutting trees and bushes.

318
00:12:26,772 --> 00:12:29,224
The space under a power line is

319
00:12:29,224 --> 00:12:30,964
not empty by accident.

320
00:12:31,144 --> 00:12:33,059
It is the room the wire needs

321
00:12:33,059 --> 00:12:34,313
on its hottest day.

322
00:12:34,663 --> 00:12:37,019
Why do power lines run at such

323
00:12:37,019 --> 00:12:41,025
dangerous voltages, hundreds of thousands of volts?

324
00:12:41,205 --> 00:12:43,353
Because it saves energy.

325
00:12:43,533 --> 00:12:45,863
The power lost in a wire depends

326
00:12:45,863 --> 00:12:47,538
on the current squared.

327
00:12:47,718 --> 00:12:50,141
If you raise the voltage ten times,

328
00:12:50,141 --> 00:12:52,355
you can send the same power with

329
00:12:52,355 --> 00:12:53,947
a tenth of the current,

330
00:12:53,947 --> 00:12:56,024
and the losses drop to about a

331
00:12:56,024 --> 00:12:56,716
hundredth.

332
00:12:57,066 --> 00:13:00,070
That is why electricity leaves the power

333
00:13:00,070 --> 00:13:03,299
plant through a transformer that raises the

334
00:13:03,299 --> 00:13:06,528
voltage, travels across the country on tall

335
00:13:06,528 --> 00:13:09,382
towers, and is then stepped down again

336
00:13:09,382 --> 00:13:11,559
in substations near the city,

337
00:13:11,559 --> 00:13:14,038
and once more on the pole outside

338
00:13:14,038 --> 00:13:14,864
your house.

339
00:13:15,044 --> 00:13:18,245
The giant towers exist because the voltage

340
00:13:18,245 --> 00:13:21,218
needs big distances from the ground and

341
00:13:21,218 --> 00:13:22,590
between the wires.

342
00:13:22,940 --> 00:13:25,241
Most lines carry three wires,

343
00:13:25,241 --> 00:13:26,590
or three bundles,

344
00:13:26,590 --> 00:13:29,764
because the grid uses three phase power,

345
00:13:29,764 --> 00:13:32,700
three currents that rise and fall one

346
00:13:32,700 --> 00:13:33,969
after the other.

347
00:13:34,149 --> 00:13:36,393
It is the most efficient way to

348
00:13:36,393 --> 00:13:38,419
move large amounts of power,

349
00:13:38,419 --> 00:13:40,590
and it is why many towers have

350
00:13:40,590 --> 00:13:42,833
their wires in groups of three.

351
00:13:43,184 --> 00:13:45,192
So here are the eleven.

352
00:13:45,372 --> 00:13:47,271
Colored balls for pilots,

353
00:13:47,271 --> 00:13:50,690
and little dumbbells that stop the vibration.

354
00:13:50,870 --> 00:13:53,094
Bundles of wires with spacers,

355
00:13:53,094 --> 00:13:55,541
insulators like stacks of plates,

356
00:13:55,541 --> 00:13:58,358
and metal rings that spread the field.

357
00:13:58,538 --> 00:14:01,846
A wire on top that catches lightning,

358
00:14:01,846 --> 00:14:03,455
spirals for birds,

359
00:14:03,455 --> 00:14:06,048
barbed wire against climbers,

360
00:14:06,048 --> 00:14:07,925
a man on a live wire,

361
00:14:07,925 --> 00:14:10,518
a sag that caused a blackout,

362
00:14:10,518 --> 00:14:13,737
and voltages high enough to save the

363
00:14:13,737 --> 00:14:14,362
energy.

364
00:14:14,713 --> 00:14:16,959
Next time you drive under a power

365
00:14:16,959 --> 00:14:17,913
line, look up,

366
00:14:17,913 --> 00:14:20,364
and you will know what everything up

367
00:14:20,364 --> 00:14:21,249
